{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1969-Filter3-oneM",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Radiant power flow and absorptance in thin films,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 423-436 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000423 - Table VI, p. 430 (Filter 3 = air (BL)^5 (LB)^5 (LH)^5 1.74B M stack 3; Stack 3 = 1.77L B (LH)^4 (BL)^5 quartz); Figs. 13, 14, 17",
  "Notes": "One-M UV bandpass filter with a single 250 A Al film at 2536 A, matching stack 3 (MgO/cryolite stack plus a group of quarter- and half-wave layers for higher rejection)\nSpecification: Peak transmittance T_max at lambda0 = 2536 A (R = 0 at lambda0); matching-stack admittance at lambda0 stated to be exactly that of stack 2.\nReported: p. 431-432: 'Owing to the position of the half-wave layer in design 3, the admittance of design 2 and design 3 are exactly the same at lambda0'; peak T very nearly T_max = 0.739 with R = 0 at lambda0; maximum rejection of filter 3 at least one optical density unit greater than the other designs (Fig. 14); passband slightly wider. Fig. 17: peak T ~0.72 at lambda0/lambda = 1.0. WARNING: as printed this design does NOT reproduce the paper: computed here T = 0.509, R = 0.174 at 253.6 nm, peak T = 0.583 at 256.5 nm; stack 3 as printed has admittance 2.04 + j1.84, not stack 2's 0.91 + j3.39. Appending (LB)^5 after the printed stack 3 (making the extra group an absentee (BL)^5(LB)^5, mirroring the air side) would restore stack 2's admittance, which suggests a misprint, but that is not printed and is not used here.\nNotes: Normal incidence assumed (no angle given). The notation is read left to right from the incident medium (air) to the substrate (quartz, n = 1.47), so layer 1 is next to air. All dielectric layers are quarter-wave optical thickness (QWOT) at lambda0 = 2536 A = 253.6 nm with constant indices; the Al film is a physical thickness with the nondispersive n - jk = 0.20 - j2.80 at 2536 A (Macleod sign convention; k = +2.80 here). B = ThOF2 n = 1.55, L = cryolite n = 1.36, H = MgO n = 1.80. Expanded as printed: (BL)^5 (10), (LB)^5 (10; the L-L junction forms a half wave, kept as two QWOT layers), (LH)^5 (10), 1.74B, M, then stack 3 = 1.77L, B, (LH)^4 (8), (BL)^5 (10): 52 layers. On the air side (BL)^5(LB)^5 is an absentee group at lambda0, so the air side is equivalent to filter 2's.\nLayers from Table VI, p. 430 (Filter 3 = air (BL)^5 (LB)^5 (LH)^5 1.74B M stack 3; Stack 3 = 1.77L B (LH)^4 (BL)^5 quartz); Figs. 13, 14, 17; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), differs from the paper: ours T 0.509, R 0.173 at 253.6 nm; paper T 0.739 with R = 0 at 253.6 nm. Table VI as printed does not give the paper’s own T 0.739 with R 0; a misprinted thickness is the likely cause.",
